 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "WEST: the rest of Guernsey. The Little Chapel, Fort Grey, the Occupation Museum and the bays on the west coast."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "HARBOUR. Ferries from the pier: SARK and the other islands. And back to Jersey and Poole when you've seen enough."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_spp_harbour",
   "title": "St Peter Port",
   "text": "The capital of Guernsey, built up a steep hillside from a harbour sheltered by Castle Cornet. The tall granite houses and narrow cobbled streets are why a Guernseyman is nicknamed a donkey, some say. A very pretty town to arrive in, by sea."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_cornet",
   "title": "Castle Cornet",
   "text": "Begun in the thirteenth century on a rock at the harbour mouth, and joined to the shore today by a breakwater. In the Civil War the castle held out for the king against Parliament for eight years, till the end of 1651, among the longest sieges in British history. At noon each day the gunners fire the Noon Gun."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_hugo",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Victor Hugo in Exile",
   "text": "The French writer Victor Hugo was banished from France after he opposed Napoleon III's seizure of power in 1851. He lived in exile in Jersey from 1852, then in Guernsey from 1855 until 1870. He called these islands 'pieces of France that fell into the sea and were picked up by England'."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_hauteville",
   "title": "Hauteville House",
   "text": "Hugo bought the house in 1856 with the money from his poems and designed the interior himself, from carved oak to blue porcelain. He wrote much of Les Misérables here, and Toilers of the Sea, set among Guernsey's fishermen. His family gave the house to the City of Paris in 1927, and it is now a museum."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_spp_market",
   "title": "The Market Halls",
   "text": "A covered market that has sold the island's produce for around two hundred years: Guernsey tomatoes, flowers, fish, local butter and golden-coloured cream. Locals queue at the dairy stall every Saturday. Bring a basket and a cheerful attitude."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_cows",
   "title": "The Guernsey Cow",
   "text": "Fawn and white, gentle and quick to make friends. Guernsey milk is a deep golden colour, because it is rich in carotene, the same pigment as in carrots. The island has kept its cattle pure for two centuries. Bringing in other cattle is banned."
  },
  {
   "x": 6,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_gache",
   "title": "Gâche",
   "text": "Gâche is the Guernsey word for a fruit loaf, a dense, buttery bread with raisins and peel, sliced thick and eaten with plenty of butter. Guernsey people make it for tea, for Christmas and for the beach. A related apple cake is called gâche mêlaïe."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_bean_jar",
   "title": "The Guernsey Bean Jar",
   "text": "A tall earthenware pot of haricot beans, pork and a pig's trotter, left to cook slowly for hours. It was a Sunday dish, put in the baker's oven on Saturday night and fetched home after church. Everybody's grandmother made the best one."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_spp_occupation",
   "title": "The Occupation",
   "text": "German forces occupied Guernsey from 30 June 1940 until 9 May 1945. Two days before, bombers hit St Peter Port harbour and killed 34 people. Thousands of children were evacuated to England, and many did not see their parents for five years. The people left behind endured shortages, curfews and deportations."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_church",
   "title": "The Town Church",
   "text": "The island's main parish church, with a spire that has guided sailors for centuries. Guernsey has ten parishes, each with its own church and its own Douzaine, a council of a dozen or more elected parish officers. The Town Church has seen weddings, funerals and the Occupation, and it's still open."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 41,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_alderney",
   "title": "A Nod to Alderney",
   "text": "The third island of the Bailiwick lies north-east, a few miles off the French coast. Alderney has puffins on Burhou, a gannet colony on its offshore rocks and a little railway run by volunteers. Much of its population was evacuated in 1940. The island's cows, the original Alderneys, gave their name to a whole breed."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "channel_islands_guernesiais",
   "title": "Guernésiais",
   "local": {
    "nrf_gg": "Guernésiais"
   },
   "text": "Guernésiais is Guernsey's own Norman French, the language of the farms, the quarries and the fishing boats for centuries. The Occupation scattered the island's children, and English took over at home, so the language is now spoken by only a small number of islanders. Marie De Garis compiled a Guernésiais dictionary, published in 1982. Bonjour is the same word as in French, but the accent is all Guernsey."
  }
 ],
